You get information a user has posted api keys,to a public git repo , and they have been compromised by an attacker, what actions do you take

Thought process
Thought process

Leaked API keys in a public repo with confirmed compromise is a "assume breach" situation — the priority order is revoke, then scope, then clean up exposure, then hunt for damage.

Immediate — kill the keys
Revoke or rotate the exposed credentials right away, before anything else. A leaked key that's already been used by an attacker is still live until you kill it, so this comes before investigation. If revocation isn't instant (some services have propagation delay), also lock down the account/permissions tied to that key in parallel.

Scope what the key could touch
Check what permissions/scopes that key had — what data, systems, or actions it could reach. Pull the provider's audit logs (AWS CloudTrail, GitHub audit log, Stripe logs, whatever the service is) for activity on that key, and look specifically for anything from unfamiliar IPs/regions or actions outside the app's normal behavior pattern, going back to whenever the key was first exposed (not just from when you noticed).

Confirm and size the compromise
Since you already know it's been compromised, figure out what the attacker actually did with it: data accessed/exfiltrated, resources created (attackers often spin up compute for crypto mining or use SES/SNS for spam), new IAM users/keys created for persistence, config changed. Attackers often create a backdoor (new access key, new user) so revoking the original key isn't enough — check for anything else they added.

Clean up the exposure itself
Remove the key from the repo, but know that git history keeps it — commit history needs rewriting (git filter-repo / BFG) or the repo needs to go private/be scrubbed, and you should treat the key as permanently burned rather than relying on history cleanup alone. Check if the repo was forked or cached anywhere (GitHub cache, Google indexing, scraper bots — these move fast, sometimes within minutes of a push).

Contain broader blast radius
Rotate any other credentials that were stored alongside it or that the compromised key had access to provision (e.g., if it's a cloud key, check for other secrets it could have read from a secrets manager). Check billing/usage dashboards for anomalous spend, which is often the fastest signal of misuse.

Root cause / prevention
Figure out how it got committed — missing .gitignore, no pre-commit secret scanning, no branch protection — and fix that gap. Add automated secret scanning (GitHub secret scanning / push protection, gitleaks, trufflehog) as a hard gate going forward.

Documentation
Log the incident: what leaked, when, exposure window, what the attacker did, remediation steps, and whether this triggers any disclosure obligation (e.g., if customer data was touched, depending on your regulatory environment).

